So, I'm Anjana Vakil, hello!

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.960
You can find me on Twitter
@AnjanaVakil

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.400
and today I'd like to talk to you about
immutable data structures

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.000
for functional programming in JavaScript.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.560
We're going to take a look at
what immutable data structures are,

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:57.900
why they're a really cool way
to handle the immutability

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.500
that we typically use when we're
doing functional programming

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.650
and how we can do that in JavaScript

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:05.950
because I hear y'all like JavaScript!

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:08.680
So, a little about me.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:12.350
I'm probably the only
not-web-developer in the room.

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:14.850
I am an engineer for Uber Research.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:19.400
I work with them to develop
a custom query language for data

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.700
in the scientific research
funding domain.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.850
I'm also an alum of the Recurse Center,

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.650
which is a fantastic programming
community in New York City,

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:31.700
and I am an alum of
the Outreachy Program,

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:32.760
which, if you have haven't heard of it,

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:33.800
is an amazing initiative

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.100
to get more women
and underrepresented folks

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.800
involved in open source projects

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.700
by offering paid internships
at organizations like Mozilla.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:42.850
So, I'm also a Mozilla community member

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.650
and a Moz tech speaker.

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:46.750
So, I am really happy
to chat about those things

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.750
if you want to come
grab me after the talk.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:54.500
But you might know that
I like functional programming.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:55.760
I think it rocks.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:59.120
Anybody else agree with me that
functional programming is cool?

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.120
Yeah!  Yeah!

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.480
So, functional programming
is a pretty great way

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.500
to avoid some of the headaches
of other paradigms

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.700
like imperative
or object-oriented programming.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.200
In functional programming,

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.600
what we typically do is conceive of our
programs as being just pure functions.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:21.100
That means that they transform
their inputs into outputs,

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:22.500
and that's all they do.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.500
They don't have any side effects,

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:26.500
things like printing to the console

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:30.100
or mutate or changing something
in the global state

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.000
would be side effect.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.800
Don’t do any of that.
None of that.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.000
So, our functions become just
pure data in, data out,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.000
like transformers of data.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:42.300
And one thing that goes hand-in-hand
with this avoidance of side effects

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:43.800
is using immutable data.

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:47.720
Immutable data meaning once
we've created it, it never changes.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:49.850
So, this is a really good way

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:53.050
to avoid accidentally changing
something outside of your function.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:55.150
If everything is immutable,
you can't change anything.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.620
So, immutability is
another thing that rocks

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:03.720
and it rocks pretty hard for some
reasons that we'll see in a moment.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.880
But speaking of rocks,
let's talk about rocks.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:08.680
So, this is a rock,

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:14.680
and immutability rocks in
the way that rocks rock.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:16.000
Now, I don't know about you,

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.300
but I've been going to a lot
of tech conferences recently

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:21.560
and I've been feeling like
there hasn't been enough poetry.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:23.060
So, I'd like to read you a poem.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:29.960
Nobody sits like this rock sits.
You rock, rock.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.560
The rock just sits, and is.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:37.880
You show us how to just sit here,
and that's what we need.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:41.080
It's so true, so deep.

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.680
This is from – Thank you.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.280
Don’t thank me, thank I Heart Huckabees,
it's a great movie.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:47.580
Check it out.  Very funny.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:51.700
So, this is really how
immutable data rocks.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:53.000
It just sits there.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:54.320
It just is.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.720
Once we've created it,
it never changes

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:58.000
and that's amazing

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.800
because it helps us avoid some
of the headaches of immutability.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:06.900
So, with immutability, we have
some things pretty easy,

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:08.200
but other things become harder

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:09.200
and we'll see how that looks.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.120
So, let's say, I have an array called
foo and it's got some numbers in it.

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:16.120
And I'm already bored.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:17.300
Okay, let's make it more fun.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:21.560
Let's say I have a zoo with some animals.
More fun!

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.160
And I decide that I want to change
something up about my zoo.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:30.000
Maybe I want to replace that rabbit there
with something a little more exotic.

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:32.000
Like an alien.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:33.600
So, this is cool.

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:36.300
I'm happy because I wanted
a more exotic zoo.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:38.000
I got an alien in my zoo now.

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:41.600
I didn't have to change anything except
for that one little cell in my array.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:43.000
That's pretty sweet

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.620
but my co-worker over there

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:52.000
was expecting zoo to be full of like
Earth beings, Earth animals,

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.150
and wasn't accounting for
there being an alien in it,

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:56.150
and it's like, who put this alien there?

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.280
Now my program doesn't work anymore.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:04:59.600
Aaah! Who did that?

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:04.600
Immutability has a couple of problems.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:08.700
We have to manage who's
been changing what, when,

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.500
who's been putting
which animals in the zoo.

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:14.400
We have to have a lot of overhead
to manage that state,

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:18.600
and that gives us headaches
as individuals and as teams.

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:21.250
We also get bugs in the code

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:23.100
because maybe I was only planning

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:27.120
or my co-worker was only planning
to handle terrestrial beings

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:30.200
and didn't have the case of
aliens accounted for,

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:31.600
and that broke something.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:37.250
So, these are some side effects of
immutability that don't make us happy.

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:39.250
Let's try doing things
the immutable way.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:41.650
So, in an immutable world,

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:48.200
my array, my zoo, once I've created it,
it just sits and is forever.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:49.400
I cannot change it.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:53.480
What I can do if I want
a new zoo that's more exotic

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:58.320
is I can make a copy that's
the same size as my original array,

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:02.320
and I can make
the modification that I want,

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:04.720
so I can put my alien in there
in place of the rabbit.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:08.880
And then I can copy over everything
else in the array that didn't change.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:13.520
So, this is pretty sweet
because now my co-worker is happy.

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:16.450
They're, like, whoo,
nothing broke in my program,

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:18.450
and it's all still Earth creatures

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:22.000
but I had to copy over
that whole array.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.500
I had to allocate the space
for the entire array,

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:28.600
even all of the stuff
that didn't change.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:30.960
I had to copy all of that over as well.

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:35.240
So, this means that
my code runs pretty slow

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:37.440
and it also takes up a lot of memory.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.440
It takes up a lot of space and time.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:41.240
The complexity on
those things are bad

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:44.440
because copying is a waste
of both time and space.

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:45.840
It makes a sad face.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:46.840
We don't want that.

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:48.940
So, if we want to do immutability,

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:51.640
we must be able to find
a better way of doing that.

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:53.440
Luckily for us,

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:55.340
a lot of very smart folks

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:57.600
have been thinking very hard
about this problem for a while,

00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:59.450
and they've come up with
some really good solutions

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:02.000
to how we can deal with
immutability efficiently.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:04.800
Immutable data structures!

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:09.300
So, immutable data structures is a term
you may have heard bandied about,

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.300
especially in conjunction
with functional programming,

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:14.300
or perhaps also with React
where they also come in very handy.

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:16.700
Technically, an immutable data structure

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:18.700
is just any data structure
that’s like the rock.

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:21.740
Once you’ve created it,
it just sits, and is.

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:23.040
It never changes.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:27.720
But also you hear the term persistent
data structures bandied about.

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:29.750
Sometimes these are
used interchangeably,

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:31.150
although they have
slightly different meanings.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:34.550
So, if immutable data is
just data that never changes,

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:37.350
persistent data structures
are ones for which

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:40.400
we have some kind of
access to old versions.

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:45.000
So, as we've been creating like new
modified versions of our data structure,

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:47.300
we keep the old versions around.

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:50.900
You might hear about partially
persistent data structures

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:53.440
where we can look at the old versions,

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:54.200
we can access them,

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:56.900
but we can't go back
and update any of them.

00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:00.760
All we can update is the
most current version that we have.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:03.680
And then you might also hear about
fully persistent data structures

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:06.350
where we can actually time travel,

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:09.640
we can go back and update
any of our past versions.

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:11.400
And if this is starting to ring a bell

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:14.200
that sounds little bit
like version control, like Git,

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:16.600
it's sort of the same idea.

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:20.500
So, we're going to talk about these as
persistent immutable data structures,

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:22.000
they're both persistent and immutable.

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:24.400
Let's see how this works.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:29.550
The key to all of this is that we want
the old versions of our data,

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:31.750
like, my original zoo to stay put.

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:33.750
We want them to
just sit like the rock

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:36.100
but we want new versions
to be created efficiently.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:44.500
So, what magical tricks do we have
to use to make this happen?

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:47.300
Do we have to make invocations?

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:51.480
Do dances to the gods of space
and time complexity?

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:52.480
No.

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:54.000
It's very simple.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:56.960
Trees and sharing.

00:08:57.760 --> 00:08:58.560
Isn't that sweet?

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:02.400
These two simple concepts will get
us efficient immutable data.

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:04.400
How?

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:08.300
So, let's talk about trees because
trees rock pretty hard as well,

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:10.880
although unfortunately I don't
have a poem for that, sorry.

00:09:13.150 --> 00:09:18.560
Imagine that we could find a way to
represent our zoo array as a tree.

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:21.850
So, one thing I could do is
I could put all of my animals,

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:24.440
all of my values,
in the leaves of a tree,

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:28.720
and I could make it so that each
leaf holds one value, one animal.

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:30.000
But they might get lonely,

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.300
so let's put them with a buddy.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:32.800
Let's put them 2 x 2.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:35.440
So, each of our leaves
will have two values

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:37.650
and we'll hope that
the buddies get along

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:39.150
and none of them eat each other,

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:42.150
looking at you, tiger, number six,
don't eat that koala.

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:46.950
And then we can connect them
together with intermediate nodes

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:47.950
and go up and up

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:51.200
until we get to the root node
that connects the whole structure,

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:57.100
and that root is now our previously array,
now represented as a tree.

00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:59.400
So, this is my zoo now
in a tree structure.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:05.200
So, given this type of structure,
how do we update something?

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:07.900
Given that my zoo is immutable,
it can never change,

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:10.640
how do I get a new version
that has my alien in it?

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:14.740
So, here what I need to do is

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:18.100
take the node that contains
the value that I want to change.

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:19.850
So, in this case that
would be the 0/1 node

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:22.520
which is on the bottom left
that you are seeing

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:25.120
and I have to copy that over

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:27.640
changing the thing that
I would like to be different.

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:30.280
So, I make a new copy
where I've still got my monkey

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:31.800
but I've changed the rabbit to an alien.

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:37.700
And then I need to copy any of
the intermediate nodes in the tree

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:40.800
that were pointing to
the node that I changed.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:45.400
So, I basically trace a path up
towards the root of the tree,

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:46.800
which now I've got a new root,

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:49.760
which means a new version
of the data structure.

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:53.800
So, this technique of
making this update

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:57.500
by copying the path from the leaf
I changed to the root

00:10:57.500 --> 00:10:58.800
is called path copying.

00:10:59.100 --> 00:10:59.800
That's pretty cool

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.760
because now I didn't have
to copy the entire array;

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:05.000
I just had to copy the nodes

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:09.600
on the way from the root
to the leaf that I changed.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:12.250
So, we've turned in something linear

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:14.350
and copying into something logarithmic.

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:16.350
That's pretty cool,
that's more performant.

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:21.800
And the beauty of this is that all
of these nodes in yellow here,

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:25.680
so most of the tree is shared
between the two versions,

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:27.280
between the old version and the new.

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:29.280
So, this saves me a lot of space

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:33.240
because I can actually reuse the parts
of the original version that didn't change,

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:35.850
whereas before, I had to
copy those over as well.

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:37.850
So, this means that

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:41.680
what was before like a lot of memory
consumption becomes much smaller

00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:45.720
because I don't have to store as many
copies of the things if they didn't change.

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:48.200
That's called structural sharing

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:51.560
because we're sharing the structure
of the tree between the two versions.

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:56.560
So, we've been talking
about updating things

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:59.960
but how do we get at
the values in our data structure?

00:12:00.650 --> 00:12:01.560
How do we access them?

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:03.660
Well, it turns out this isn't just a tree,

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:08.050
it's a special type of tree
called a TRIE tree,

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:10.840
which originally came
from the world "retrieval,"

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:13.650
so people I guess
could pronounce it tree

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:17.040
but that’s confusing because
we also pronounce T-R-E-E tree,

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:19.040
so we are going to call them "tries"

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:20.040
because that's sort of
what it looks like.

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:21.440
So, our tree is a trie.

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:23.140
That’s cool.

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:29.640
A trie is a type of tree where the leaves
hold the values of our data as we saw,

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:32.000
and the paths from the root to the values

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:36.300
represent the keys that
that data is associated with.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:41.680
So, often you see tries used for
storing things like words as keys.

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:45.600
So, for example, if I have
the word T as one of my keys,

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:48.500
what I do to get to the value
associated with T

00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:50.800
is I traverse the tree
one letter at a time.

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:53.000
So, from the root I go to T,

00:12:53.150 --> 00:12:54.440
then I go to E,

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:55.540
and then I go to A,

00:12:55.800 --> 00:12:57.600
and I get TEA as my key,

00:12:57.600 --> 00:12:59.600
and my value there is 3

00:13:00.150 --> 00:13:03.560
because everything else sound like "e"
in this talk, for some reason.

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:06.450
So, this is pretty cool,

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:10.100
but in our data structure,
we weren't using words as keys,

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:14.320
we just wanted an array-type thing,
we wanted indices, right?

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:20.440
So, the insight here is that if we treat
the index as a binary number,

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:23.560
then we can pretend that
that's kind of like our “word”

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:26.360
and we can descend
the tree bit-by-bit

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:31.040
as if each bit in our binary representation
of our number is a letter.

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:33.340
So, let's see how that works.

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:37.000
If I'm trying to get at
item 5 in my array,

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:39.000
so the animal at index 5,

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:41.400
I'd convert that to binary,

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:43.000
so that's 101,

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:46.580
and then I step through
that as if it was a word.

00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:49.520
I step through it letter-by-letter,
bit-by-bit.

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:54.000
So, I go from the root to the branch.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:55.640
I have a choice of either 0 or 1.

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:57.140
I go to branch 1 first.

00:13:57.450 --> 00:13:59.000
Then I go to branch 0,

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:03.000
and then I take
the thing on the 1 side.

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:05.760
So, I go 1, 0, 1 down my tree

00:14:05.860 --> 00:14:08.300
and I end up at my frog in index 5.

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:12.950
So, this is a pretty simple insight

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:14.950
but it ends up being
incredibly powerful

00:14:14.950 --> 00:14:20.150
because it allows us to quickly
traverse this tree structure,

00:14:20.150 --> 00:14:22.550
which lets us use that structural sharing

00:14:22.750 --> 00:14:27.000
to more efficiently represent our new copies
of our immutable data structure.

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:32.700
And importantly, we don't have
to be using a binary tree,

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:35.560
meaning we have two
branches from each node.

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:38.700
That fits pretty well on a slide,

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:42.360
but actually what you mostly
see is a 32-way branching.

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:44.650
So, in our trees that
we've been looking at,

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:48.160
we've kind of had one bit
of information per level.

00:14:48.460 --> 00:14:50.880
And we've been descending bit-by-bit,

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:53.840
but if we had a 32-way
branching tree,

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:56.150
it would be five bits of information

00:14:56.150 --> 00:14:58.150
that we would be
representing at each level.

00:14:58.550 --> 00:15:00.350
So, that would look
something like this.

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:04.600
If we had a much bigger number,
like 18977,

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:09.160
in binary that's that
bunch of 1s and 0s,

00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:11.450
this would be a really deep tree

00:15:11.450 --> 00:15:13.600
if I had to descend
it one bit at a time, right?

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:15.600
It would be like 15-levels deep.

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:17.000
Too much, too long.

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:20.480
So, if I'd make more
branches at each level,

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:26.200
then I can chunk this up into kind
of 5-bit letters as it were,

00:15:26.350 --> 00:15:30.080
and descend the tree
that’s now only three levels

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:34.320
using the 32-way branching.

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:40.000
So, this is kind of a tradeoff between
how deep your tree is going to be

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:42.000
and how big the nodes
are going to be

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:45.100
because if I have just one bit
of information at each level

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:46.280
then I have really small nodes.

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:47.600
That's quick to copy over,

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:50.760
but I have to go very, very deep
down the tree for a larger array.

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:55.500
And generally, research has found
that 32 tends to be a pretty good tradeoff

00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:57.500
between the size of the nodes
and the depth of the tree.

00:15:58.900 --> 00:16:04.400
Okay.  So, we've just seen what's
called often a Bitmapped Vector Trie.

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:07.160
Sometimes also called a
Bit Partitioned Vector Trie.

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:08.300
That's just jargon.

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:09.400
We don't need to care about that.

00:16:09.400 --> 00:16:11.600
But if you want something to Google,
you can Google that.

00:16:13.450 --> 00:16:16.200
This is cool for array-type things,

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:18.400
where we have an index
that we want to jump to,

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:19.600
but what about objects?

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:24.720
We also want to be able to associate
values with arbitrary keys, not just indices,

00:16:25.100 --> 00:16:26.920
so we want non-integers as keys.

00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:27.920
How does that work?

00:16:29.720 --> 00:16:32.800
So, if I want a version of my data structure
where it's no longer an array

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:34.400
but it's something like an object

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:37.200
where I'm associating letters
with each of my animals,

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:39.840
like M for monkey,
and P for panda, etc.,

00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:43.160
what I can do is I can take my keys,

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:44.560
in this case, they're letters,

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:47.800
and hash them to get a number
that represents the key,

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:52.400
so that each key will
have its own number.

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:54.700
They won't be in order necessarily,
but that's okay.

00:16:54.700 --> 00:16:56.000
Objects don't have to be in order.

00:16:56.350 --> 00:16:59.960
And then we can use the hash
of that number in binary

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:02.200
to descend the tree as before.

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:07.320
So, if I wanted to look up
the value associated with key "F,"

00:17:07.620 --> 00:17:10.000
I could hash F, get some number,

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:11.300
let's say I get 5,

00:17:11.300 --> 00:17:13.800
like, A, B, C, D, E, 5,

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:16.650
that would be represented
in binary as 101,

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:18.650
and again I descend the tree as before.

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:22.320
Here, for simplicity,
just using a one bit at a time,

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:23.400
two-way branching tree.

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:27.160
But typically, we would be doing
this with 32 branches per level.

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:34.600
So, again, we just descend the tree using
the binary representation of our key.

00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:36.900
In this case, we used
a hash function to transform it

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:39.920
from some arbitrary object
into a number

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:42.300
and we get the animal we want.

00:17:42.300 --> 00:17:43.300
In this case, our frog.

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:45.400
Cool.

00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:47.400
So, that, if you want to Google it,

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:50.640
the thing you could Google is
a Hash Array Mapped Trie.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:55.200
And this is a data structure that was
sort of pioneered by Phil Bagwell,

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:57.580
and then Rich Hickey
implemented them in Clojure,

00:17:57.580 --> 00:17:59.180
and a lot of what I have
been talking about

00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:02.900
is kind of inspired by work
in languages like Clojure

00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:04.900
to implement these
data structures efficiently.

00:18:05.500 --> 00:18:09.600
There's a ton of other optimizations

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:12.000
that are usually done on
these data structures

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:13.500
to make them super-duper fast

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:16.400
and lots of details that
we're not covering here,

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:18.400
but this is the basic idea.

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:20.900
Trees to represent our data,

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:22.400
structural sharing

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:25.600
so that we can reuse as much
information as possible

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:28.100
between the old versions
and the new versions,

00:18:28.550 --> 00:18:34.300
and this idea of using binary
representations of our keys,

00:18:34.300 --> 00:18:38.080
whether indices or hashed keys,

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:41.160
to descend the tree
to find the thing we're looking for.

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:47.320
So, to recap, mutability induces headaches.

00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:50.600
It is to be avoided especially if
you're doing functional programming

00:18:50.600 --> 00:18:54.640
where the essential idea
is to not have side effects

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:59.240
and only be using pure functions
that don't change anything

00:18:59.240 --> 00:19:02.800
except do the computation on
their input and return a new output.

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:05.600
Immutability, on the other hand, is great

00:19:05.750 --> 00:19:07.200
because when I'm using immutable data,

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:09.880
I can't mess up my
co-worker's program

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:13.680
by making the zoo that she
thought was all Earth animals

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:15.000
suddenly have an alien in it.

00:19:15.700 --> 00:19:19.000
But copying is a really bad way
of handling immutable data

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:23.480
because it is not efficient neither with
respect to time, nor with space.

00:19:24.180 --> 00:19:25.680
And structural sharing,

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:28.400
using these tree structures
or trie structures,

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:29.900
and using structural sharing

00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:33.080
to save as much information as possible
from one version to the next

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:35.080
is the really performant way to do this.

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:39.850
So, you're probably thinking, okay,
these data structures are cool

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:42.700
but what am I supposed to do with them?

00:19:42.700 --> 00:19:47.280
I'm not going to be like building
boxes of emoji here, am I?

00:19:47.480 --> 00:19:48.580
No, you don't have to.

00:19:50.200 --> 00:19:53.900
In JavaScript, there are some
really great libraries out there

00:19:53.900 --> 00:19:56.720
to help you use these right off the bat.

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:58.400
There are various solutions

00:19:58.400 --> 00:20:00.000
but I'm going to talk about
a couple of them.

00:20:00.650 --> 00:20:02.000
So, one is called mori.

00:20:02.450 --> 00:20:06.920
Mori is basically a port of
ClojureScript by David Nolen

00:20:07.150 --> 00:20:11.000
that allows you to leverage the
implementations of these data structures

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:12.200
from ClojureScript, 

00:20:12.200 --> 00:20:15.400
which is the version of Clojure
that targets JavaScript

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:19.720
from the comfort of your
vanilla JavaScript.

00:20:20.720 --> 00:20:24.200
And it's got a bit more
of a Clojure feel to it,

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:26.200
little bit more of a
functional language feel.

00:20:26.650 --> 00:20:28.000
The API is functional

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:29.600
and we're going to see what
that looks like in a moment.

00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:32.960
But that's one thing that kind
of sets this library apart.

00:20:33.860 --> 00:20:36.200
On the other hand,
there's also Immutable.js.

00:20:36.700 --> 00:20:39.100
This is a library put out by Facebook.

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:40.800
It was created by Lee Byron.

00:20:41.200 --> 00:20:45.160
And this is a JavaScript implementation
of these data structures.

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:48.600
So, it has a bit more of that
native JavaScript feel to it.

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:52.000
It doesn't have the kind of the
Clojure background brought in.

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:55.800
And that means it's got a more
object-oriented style API,

00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:59.600
although it is still returning
new versions of data structures

00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:04.120
instead of changing
mutable structures in place.

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:07.200
So, let's see what those look like.

00:21:07.900 --> 00:21:11.100
This is how you might use mori
to create what's called a vector.

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:13.820
A vector is the data
structure from mori

00:21:13.820 --> 00:21:18.300
that you'd probably be using
as an array-type thing.

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:23.200
So, I've got a vector that I'm calling 'a'
because it's sort of array-ish.

00:21:23.600 --> 00:21:25.200
It's got 1 and 2 in it.

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:28.500
And if I want to push
something onto that,

00:21:29.100 --> 00:21:31.100
the function that I'd use is conj.

00:21:31.100 --> 00:21:33.800
This is from the Clojure world
lisp-speak.

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:38.780
And what I would pass in to the
conj function is the original copy a,

00:21:38.980 --> 00:21:41.480
and the thing I want
to add on to the end,

00:21:41.480 --> 00:21:42.480
so in this case, 3.

00:21:43.700 --> 00:21:46.720
And you'll see that that creates
a new data structure on the right.

00:21:47.450 --> 00:21:50.120
These vectors [1 2] and [1 2 3],

00:21:50.450 --> 00:21:53.300
they look a little bit different
than regular JavaScript arrays

00:21:53.300 --> 00:21:55.650
because they are Clojure vectors,

00:21:55.650 --> 00:21:56.950
they are not really JavaScript arrays,

00:21:56.950 --> 00:21:58.950
although you can convert back and forth.

00:21:59.950 --> 00:22:03.300
But the point is that this conj
function returns a new value

00:22:03.300 --> 00:22:05.300
which then I can catch as a2

00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:07.000
I can capture that,

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:10.000
and I can prove to myself
that my original a didn't change

00:22:10.400 --> 00:22:12.800
by using the count function to
see how many things are in it.

00:22:12.800 --> 00:22:14.400
There's only two things still in it.

00:22:14.900 --> 00:22:19.240
But I can prove to myself that my
version, a2, has the third thing

00:22:19.480 --> 00:22:20.500
by trying to access,

00:22:20.500 --> 00:22:23.700
by using the get function to
access the item at index 2,

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:24.400
the third thing,

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:26.400
which it tells me is indeed 3.

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:27.400
Cool.

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:31.400
This is how you would do
the same thing in Immutable.js.

00:22:32.150 --> 00:22:36.440
Here you would use Immutable.js.list.of,

00:22:36.540 --> 00:22:38.640
it's a little interesting syntax there.

00:22:39.400 --> 00:22:43.700
And there it creates something that
looks a bit more like a JavaScript array

00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:45.800
although it is not a normal array,

00:22:45.800 --> 00:22:48.000
it is an immutablejs.list.

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:50.780
That I'll call a

00:22:50.780 --> 00:22:55.160
and then if I want to add something
onto a new version of a,

00:22:55.400 --> 00:22:59.600
I use this sort of dot-method
notation that we're used to.

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:00.900
I'd say a.push(3),

00:23:00.900 --> 00:23:03.750
but, importantly,
this is still not changing a.

00:23:03.750 --> 00:23:06.000
It kind of feels like it should be
but it's not,

00:23:06.350 --> 00:23:10.160
it's just returning a new version of a,

00:23:10.160 --> 00:23:12.160
which I'm going to capture as a2

00:23:12.550 --> 00:23:14.660
and I can prove it to myself
that a didn't change.

00:23:14.660 --> 00:23:16.660
a.size tells me it's still 2,

00:23:17.050 --> 00:23:20.560
and if I try to get the item
at index 2 from a2,

00:23:21.300 --> 00:23:23.840
I find that it's 3, as I expected. 

00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:29.700
So, similarly, for what are called maps,

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:34.320
which is kind of the key-value object
that we might be using,

00:23:35.220 --> 00:23:36.850
if I create an object o,

00:23:36.850 --> 00:23:39.360
which is going to be my
mori.hashMap data structure,

00:23:39.460 --> 00:23:41.360
I'm associating a with 1, b with 2,

00:23:42.550 --> 00:23:45.100
again, we see that the syntax
for these is a little bit different

00:23:45.100 --> 00:23:46.700
than our regular JavaScript objects

00:23:46.700 --> 00:23:48.550
because they are not
regular JavaScript objects.

00:23:48.550 --> 00:23:50.850
They're super special
immutable data structures,

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:52.250
they need special syntax.

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:57.300
So, if I want to change
the value of one of my keys,

00:23:57.300 --> 00:23:59.000
I can use this asoc function,

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:01.500
pass in my original map o,

00:24:01.700 --> 00:24:05.300
and then change the value of
a to 3 in my new version o2,

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:08.300
and again I can prove to myself
that the original didn't change

00:24:08.400 --> 00:24:10.300
by using the get function

00:24:10.300 --> 00:24:14.400
to make sure that a in
the original one o, is 1,

00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:16.400
and a in o2 is 3,

00:24:16.650 --> 00:24:17.650
as I would expect.

00:24:18.200 --> 00:24:20.800
And it looks quite similar
in Immutable.js

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:23.200
except the data structure
is called Map, not hashMap,

00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:26.760
and I can pass in a little object,
a little JavaScript object,

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:29.360
and it creates me a Map o,

00:24:29.400 --> 00:24:33.100
that looks again little bit more
JavaScript syntax that we're used to.

00:24:33.100 --> 00:24:36.450
This has a bit more of
a syntax and a feel

00:24:36.450 --> 00:24:38.700
that you might be used to from
JavaScript programming.

00:24:39.250 --> 00:24:44.680
I can use the set method on o to create
a new version where a is now 3,

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:49.100
and I can use the get methods on my
old version o, and my new version o2

00:24:49.150 --> 00:24:52.240
to prove to myself that
the old one didn't change.

00:24:52.640 --> 00:24:54.720
So, these are really
immutable data structures.

00:24:55.150 --> 00:25:00.840
They look really weird if you try
to look at them in the console

00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:02.120
just as JavaScript objects.

00:25:02.120 --> 00:25:03.800
They're really fun to
kind of poke down into

00:25:03.800 --> 00:25:05.800
because they have this
complicated tree structure.

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:08.050
So, I highly recommend
you try out these libraries

00:25:08.050 --> 00:25:09.450
and see what works for you.

00:25:09.700 --> 00:25:13.000
I can tell you really just briefly
before I run out of time here,

00:25:13.850 --> 00:25:18.720
that how they compare is basically,
again, mori is from the Clojure world,

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:19.720
it's ClojureScript.

00:25:19.900 --> 00:25:22.640
Immutable.js is from the JavaScript world.

00:25:23.900 --> 00:25:27.100
That means the Immutable.js
has this more object oriented,

00:25:27.100 --> 00:25:29.800
o.get kind of feel to it,

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:31.500
which might be comfortable

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:34.320
if you are used to writing
JavaScript that looks like that.

00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:36.320
However, for me, I find it a little bit...

00:25:36.320 --> 00:25:38.420
it gives me a little bit of
a cognitive dissonance there

00:25:38.420 --> 00:25:41.920
because it looks like we're
mutating things with that method,

00:25:42.220 --> 00:25:43.100
those method calls.

00:25:43.250 --> 00:25:44.500
We're not, we're not,

00:25:44.850 --> 00:25:48.000
but for me, to get more into the
mindset of functional programming,

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:50.720
I prefer the functional API of Mori

00:25:50.920 --> 00:25:52.720
because we are really
getting used to

00:25:52.720 --> 00:25:55.800
the way that we want to conceive
of everything as pure functions

00:25:55.800 --> 00:25:57.800
that just take inputs
and return outputs.

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:01.000
We don't even want to be
kind of in the mindset

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:04.640
of making changes
in place to objects.

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:08.440
There's also some minor performance
differences between the two,

00:26:08.440 --> 00:26:11.000
Mori is a bit faster,
Immutable.js is a bit smaller.

00:26:11.450 --> 00:26:13.000
But they're both great options,

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:14.000
try them out,

00:26:14.300 --> 00:26:16.000
hope one of them works for you.

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:17.800
So, that's my talk.

00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:19.600
I hope it's been useful.

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:22.000
Go forth and don't mutate your data.

00:26:23.150 --> 00:26:24.680
Here's some references for you.

00:26:25.300 --> 00:26:25.780
Thanks.

